WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.200
Elsewhere in the course, we established a&nbsp;
relationship between the Person.CountryRegion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:12.560
and Person.StateProvince tables, with the&nbsp;
CountryRegionCode column, the primary key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.000
in the Person.CountryRegion table, being linked&nbsp;
to the foreign key field of the same field name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.840
in the Person.StateProvince table. Let's&nbsp;
look at one aspect of this relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:30.320
from a practical standpoint. I still have the&nbsp;
design of the Person.StateProvince table open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.880
and I am going to select the Table&nbsp;
Designer menu and the Relationships option.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.480
Then, I will select the relationship we created,&nbsp;
and I just want you to notice one setting:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:51.760
the Enforce Foreign Key Constraint option&nbsp;
is set to Yes. That is the default setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.120
when a relationship is created. This setting&nbsp;
enforces a concept called referential integrity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:03.120
which ensures that a primary key value exists in&nbsp;
a relationship before the corresponding foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:10.880
key value is allowed. That is the technical&nbsp;
definition of referential integrity. Let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:17.840
now explain this through the lens of looking at&nbsp;
the Person.CountryRegion table. I've closed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:24.880
Foreign Key Relationships window and then return&nbsp;
the top 1000 rows from the Person.CountryRegion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:32.160
table. Let me now pose a question: Should a&nbsp;
country region code be defined in this table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.840
before it is allowed anywhere else? In other&nbsp;
words, what do you think would happen if in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:50.880
Person.StateProvince table, a CountryRegionCode&nbsp;
was entered that did not exist here? As you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:57.280
imagine, we would have a problem because we&nbsp;
would have no idea what the CountryRegionCode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:04.800
should represent. This situation causes orphaned&nbsp;
records, which are records in a foreign key table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:13.120
with no matches in a primary key table.&nbsp;
Enforcing referential integrity ensures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:20.000
this does not happen, that a primary key&nbsp;
value must exist for it to be allowed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
any foreign key table. In other words,&nbsp;
only CountryRegionCodes that exist here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.560
are allowed in any entity to which this&nbsp;
has a relationship using this field.

